git-state changes. Reproduced independently (not trusted from manifest):
`go build ./...` clean; `bash .swarm/tier3/ND1/accept.sh` → ORACLE PASS;
`gofmt -l` clean on all three touched files; `go test -count=1
./internal/services/dataloader/... ./internal/services/mcpsvc/...` all ok.

Design-fidelity check (line-by-line against ND-RUN-SPEC.md's FIXED design):
- Gap A: `hasPendingPostedAffinity` factored out and reused byte-identically
  on Description first, OriginalDescription second; empty-normalized-string
  guard preserved on both calls. Matches spec exactly.
- Gap B: `isScheduledSettledPair` checked last in `isCandidatePair`; 7-day
  window + same account + exactly-one-scheduled/other-posted + check-shape
  exclusion on both sides + >=2 shared-token gate, tokenizer (lowercase,
  split on non a-z0-9 runs, drop <3 bytes) and stopword list transcribed
  verbatim (17/17 words match spec's list). `pairKey`, greedy pairing,
  `classify()`, status keyword lists confirmed untouched via diff.
- A5: `duplicates.go` diff is prose-only (`git diff --stat`: 5 insertions,
  3 deletions, no code lines), contains "autopay" (`grep -n autopay`).

Adversarial probes run (throwaway, in the copy only):
1. Tokenization corner case: `tokenizeDescription("USAA P&C AUTOPAY")` →
   `["usaa"]` only ("p","c" <3 bytes dropped, "autopay" stopword-dropped)
   — matches the spec's own insurance-pair calibration math (scheduled
   side contributes {usaa,insurance}, this string alone can't source the
   2nd token, consistent with the real fixture pairing off "USAA Property
   and Casualty Insurance" not "USAA P&C AUTOPAY").
2. Realistic false-positive construction: "Discover Card Payment"
   (Scheduled, $35, day 1) vs "Discover Card Minimum Payment" (Posted, $35,
   day 6) — two hypothetical DISTINCT real charges — DO share 2 tokens
   ("discover","card") and DO get paired (`sharedTokenCount`=2, 1 pair
   found). This is a genuine heuristic false-positive class the fixed
   design's guard list (Google One/Google.com, Wire Fee/Monroe, single-
   token Lucid) does not cover. Not grounds for FAIL: it's not one of
   A1-A5's named guards, the design is explicitly "fixed — do not
   redesign," and spec §9 already documents these constants as
   v1-hardcoded pending real false-positive data. Reported as an
   observation below.
3. Order-dependence / partner-theft probe: built a 3-way collision (a
   Pending row, its true Posted settlement 2 days later, and a fabricated
   Scheduled "decoy" 1 day from the Pending row sharing 2 tokens with the
   Posted row). With array order [pending, posted, decoy] and
   [pending, decoy, posted] (realistic date-ascending order matching real
   loader output), the correct pair wins because the earliest-dated row is
   processed first in the per-i greedy loop and claims its only valid
   candidate before the decoy competes. With array order
   [decoy, posted, pending] the decoy DOES steal the posted row, leaving
   the true pending duplicate undetected — confirmed live
   (TestProbe_GreedyStealAcrossShapes_ReversedOrder). This is real but is
   a property of the pre-existing per-index greedy pairing loop (identical
   before/after this diff — confirmed via `git diff`, and the task's own
   "no other behavior changes: ... greedy pairing ... untouched" scope
   line), which Gap-B merely exercises through a wider candidate set; it
   is not something ND1 could fix without violating "do not redesign."
   Reported as an observation.
4. Real-data re-pairing regression check (the strongest test): diffed
   `UnresolvedDuplicates()+ResolvedDuplicates()+KeptBothDuplicates()`
   (pair_key + both sides' date/desc/amount) between master's
   near_duplicates.go and this diff's, both run over the identical live
   `data/` copy. Result: 17 total pairs before → 24 after, and the ONLY
   difference is the addition of exactly the 7 expected new UNRESOLVED
   pairs (all matching spec's list verbatim). Every one of the 16
   RESOLVED and 1 KEPTBOTH pair_keys is byte-identical before and after —
   direct proof of no re-pairing regression and no re-pairing of the
   Lucid ($1580.43, 4 checks + 2 scheduled) or insurance ($464.99) buckets
   specifically requested for probing.
5. `isScheduledSettledPair` called directly (bypassing the outflow/sign
   pre-filter) on a positive-amount pair still returns true — confirmed
   unreachable through `detectNearDuplicatePairs` (line ~92 filters
   `t.Amount >= 0` before bucketing), so not a live defect.
6. Both-empty-status pair: 0 pairs, matches `isScheduledStatus` requiring
   non-empty.

All A1-A5 criteria independently verified against evidence, not just the
manifest's claims. No criterion is violated.

OBSERVATIONS (backlog, not FAIL grounds — outside ND1's fixed-design scope):
- Gap-B's 2-shared-token / stopword-list heuristic will false-positive on
  realistic fixed-amount recurring bills whose merchant name yields 2+
  non-generic tokens shared across two genuinely distinct charges within 7
  days (demonstrated: "Discover Card Payment"/"Discover Card Minimum
  Payment"). Spec §9 already flags all these constants as v1-hardcoded
  pending real false-positive data; this is evidence for that future
  tuning pass, not a defect in this task.
- The near-duplicate greedy pairing algorithm (unchanged by ND1) is
  per-array-index order-dependent rather than a global-minimum matching;
  adding any new candidate shape (Gap A's fallback or Gap B) widens the
  set of transactions that can compete for a partner, so an adversarially
  ordered future dataset could have a weaker-signal shape steal a partner
  from a stronger one. Confirmed inert on the current real dataset (item
  4). Worth a follow-up task if greedy pairing is ever revisited — out of
  ND1's explicit scope ("greedy pairing ... untouched").
